    :: the river. J. B. CARR, Brig a (Her- Genera I. NORFOLK, VA., March 15, 1865 — 6.30 p. in. Major-General ORD, Army of the James: Army gun-boats can go to Suffolk. Cavalry can land at several points on the Xansemond; gun-boats are not necessary to cover them anywhere on the Xansemond. If intended to cross the Blackwater anywhere near Franklin or South Quay pontoons or a flat or raft are necessary. A man named Boyle, who always accompanies expeditions in that direction, is now at City Point a contractor to get out railroad ties. I will not come up until to morrow, and will leave word where the landing should be for Sumner's cavalry, if I am not here to direct. I am sure that anywhere near the direction of Xottoway railroad bridge from Suffolk boats are necessary to cross the river. On the upper Blackwater and upper Xottoway crossings might be effected without pontoons. Colonel Lewis has about 500 cavalry; that is all I have this side of the James. Bo
